'''Michael Gary Schietzelt, Jr.''' (born October 24, 1986) is an American attorney and [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] politician representing the [[North Carolina's 35th House district]] since 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Representative Mike Schietzelt - Biography - North Carolina General Assembly |url=https://www.ncleg.gov/Members/Biography/H/840 |access-date=2025-04-27 |website=www.ncleg.gov}}</ref> A former United States Marine and Duke Law School graduate, Schietzelt was first elected in the [[2024 North Carolina House of Representatives election|2024 general election]] and assumed office on January 1, 2025.<ref name="NCGA">{{cite web |title=Representative Mike Schietzelt (Republican, 35th District) |url=https://www.ncleg.gov/Members/Biography/H/820 |access-date=25 October 2025 |publisher=North Carolina General Assembly}}</ref><ref name="Ballotpedia">{{cite web |title=Mike Schietzelt |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Mike_Schietzelt |access-date=25 October 2025 |website=Ballotpedia}}</ref>

== Early life, education, and career ==
Schietzelt was born on October 24, 1986 in [[Escondido, California]]. He was raised in [[Burlington, North Carolina]] by his father, Michael, Sr., a [[United States Marine Corps|United States Marine]] and a [[Vietnam War]] veteran.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2023-11-24 |title=Home - Mike Schietzelt For 35 |url=https://mikeschietzelt.com/ |access-date=2025-10-24 |language=en-US}}</ref> Schietzelt graduated from [[Western Alamance High School]] in 2004 and earned a bachelor's degree from the [[University of North Carolina at Greensboro]], majoring in Music Performance as a trumpet player.<ref name=":0" /> Schietzelt performed at amusement parks and on cruise ships before enlisting in the United States Marine Corps in 2011 as a member of "The Commandant's Own" [[United States Marine Drum and Bugle Corps]]. In the Marine Corps, Schietzelt performed for many top-ranking officials in the United States government and sounded taps for military funerals at [[Arlington National Cemetery]].<ref name="auto" />

After completing active service, Schietzelt attended [[Duke University School of Law]] in [[Durham, North Carolina]] and earned a [[Juris Doctor]] degree in 2018.<ref name="auto" /> Upon graduation, he served as a law clerk to [[Mark Martin (judge)|Chief Justice Mark D. Martin]] of the [[North Carolina Supreme Court]].

Schietzelt worked as a Criminal Justice Fellow with the [[John Locke Foundation]] in [[Raleigh, North Carolina]] from 2019 to 2020, researching and publishing on issues related to the criminal code.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2021-11-08 |title=Mike Schietzelt - John Locke Foundation |url=https://www.johnlocke.org/guest-voice/mike-schietzelt/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240917024102/https://www.johnlocke.org/guest-voice/mike-schietzelt/ |archive-date=2024-09-17 |access-date=2025-10-24 |work=John Locke Foundation |language=en-US}}</ref> He argued for structural reforms to the criminal law that would ease burdens on law enforcement and the court system, "protect[ ] the rule of law and boost[ ] public trust and confidence in our criminal justice system."<ref>{{Cite news |date=2020-04-03 |title=Criminal Law Reform In North Carolina |url=https://www.johnlocke.org/research/criminal-law-reform-in-north-carolina/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250122020036/https://www.johnlocke.org/research/criminal-law-reform-in-north-carolina/ |archive-date=2025-01-22 |access-date=2025-10-24 |work=John Locke Foundation |language=en-US}}</ref>

In March 2020, Schietzelt became the founding Constitutional Law Fellow of [[Regent University School of Law]]'s Robertson Center for Constitutional Law. Schietzelt represented members of [[United States Congress|Congress]], campus ministries, and other faith-based organizations in the Supreme Court of the United States and United States Circuit Courts of Appeals as [[Amicus curiae|amici curiae]] in cases implicating the First Amendment and separation of powers.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Mike Schietzelt, J.D. |url=https://www.regent.edu/faculty/mike-schietzelt-j-d/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220319160907/https://www.regent.edu/faculty/mike-schietzelt-j-d/ |archive-date=2022-03-19 |access-date=2025-10-24 |work=Regent University |language=en-US}}</ref> A Lecturer at Regent, Schietzelt taught courses on the [[First Amendment to the United States Constitution|First Amendment]] and Religious Liberty. He also published scholarly articles on constitutional issues, most notably the [[Free Exercise Clause]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Lingo |first=Bradley J. |last2=Schietzelt |first2=Michael G. |date=September 22, 2022 |title=A Second-Class First Amendment Right? Text, Structure, History, and Free Exercise After Fulton |url=https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4044068 |journal=[[Wake Forest Law Review]] |volume=57 |issue=3 |pages=711–775 |via=SSRN}}</ref>

In July 2022, Schietzelt joined the law firm [[Michael Best|Michael Best and Friedrich, LLP]] as a member of the litigation practice group.

== Political experience ==
Schietzelt announced his campaign for the 35th District of the [[North Carolina House of Representatives]] in December 2023. He won the Republican Party [[primary election]] on March 5, 2024,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-04-01 |title=2024 NC Republican Primary Election Results - State House District 35 |url=https://www.fayobserver.com/elections/results/2024-03-05/race/35357/north-carolina |access-date=2025-10-24 |website=Fayetteville Observer |language=en}}</ref> and he won the [[general election]] on November 5, 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-12-13 |title=2024 NC General Election Results - State House District 35 |url=https://www.usatoday.com/elections/results/2024-11-05/race/35047/north-carolina |access-date=2025-10-24 |website=USA TODAY |language=en}}</ref> He was sworn in as a member of the House of Representatives in January 2025 by the judge for whom he clerked, retired Chief Justice [[Mark Martin (judge)|Mark Martin]], now dean of the Kenneth F. Kahn School of Law at [[High Point University]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=It was a special moment being sworn into office by the same person who swore me into the bar as a young attorney. Thank you, Chief. {{!}} Mike Schietzelt {{!}} 30 comments |url=https://www.linkedin.com/posts/michaelschietzelt_it-was-a-special-moment-being-sworn-into-activity-7284366419029286912-EhDe |access-date=2025-10-24 |website=www.linkedin.com |language=en}}</ref>

In May 2025, Schietzelt introduced an amendment to the House budget prohibiting the use of state funds to convert the section of [[U.S. Route 1 in North Carolina|U.S. 1]]/[[Capital Boulevard]] between Raleigh and Wake Forest into a toll road.<ref>{{cite news |date=22 May 2025 |title=NC House moves to prevent tolls on Capital Boulevard |url=https://www.yahoo.com/news/nc-house-moves-prevent-tolls-211702033.html |access-date=25 October 2025 |work=Yahoo News}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=23 May 2025 |title=NC lawmakers pump the brakes on tolling plan for Wake County |url=https://www.wral.com/story/toll-road-s-dead-nc-lawmakers-pump-the-brakes-on-road-widening-plan-for-wake-county/22030372/ |access-date=25 October 2025 |work=WRAL}}</ref>  He argued that tolling the highway would impose an unfair cost on working- and middle-class commuters in his district and that a roadway free to the public for over a century should remain so.<ref>{{cite news |date=22 May 2025 |title=Debate continues over toll road proposal on Capital Boulevard |url=https://www.axios.com/local/raleigh/2025/05/22/toll-road-capital-boulevard-raleigh-wake-forest-general-assembly |access-date=25 October 2025 |work=Axios Raleigh}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=14 July 2025 |title=NC road upgrades face opposition over tolling plan |url=https://www.wunc.org/term/news/2025-07-14/nc-road-upgrades-tolls-capital-boulevard |access-date=25 October 2025 |work=WUNC}}</ref>  The House adopted the amendment by a bipartisan 71–37 vote, reflecting broad legislative resistance to the tolling proposal, although the measure still required Senate concurrence and inclusion in the final budget to take effect.

